**while Loop:**

A while loop is an **entry-controlled** loop that repeats code while a condition is **true**.

**Key Characteristics:**

1. **Entry-controlled:**
   - Condition checked **before** each iteration
   - May **not execute** at all if condition is initially false

2. **Syntax:**
   ```java
   while (condition) {
       // Code to repeat
       // Update condition to avoid infinite loop
   }
   ```

3. **Use Cases:**
   - Unknown number of iterations
   - Loop until specific condition met
   - User input validation
   - Reading data until end marker

**Example:**
```java
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    System.out.println(i);  // Prints 1 to 5
    i++;  // Must update to avoid infinite loop
}
```

**Common Mistake:**
```java
// WRONG - Infinite loop
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    // Forgot i++!
}
```

**Key Point:** Always update the loop variable inside the body to eventually make the condition false.

code:
// Valid input loop
int number = 0;
while (number <= 0) {
    System.out.print("Enter positive number: ");
    number = scanner.nextInt();
    if (number <= 0) {
        System.out.println("Invalid! Try again.");
    }
}
System.out.println("You entered: " + number);

