**Enhanced for Loop (for-each):**

**Syntax:**
```java
for (dataType variable : array) {
    // Use variable
}
```

**Advantages:**
- ✅ Cleaner, more readable code
- ✅ No index management
- ✅ No off-by-one errors
- ✅ Slightly faster than indexed loop

**Example:**
```java
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

// Enhanced for loop
for (int num : numbers) {
    System.out.println(num);  // Simple!
}

// vs. Traditional for loop
for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(numbers[i]);  // More verbose
}
```

**Limitations:**

1. **Read-only** - Cannot modify array elements:
```java
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
for (int num : arr) {
    num = num * 2;  // Doesn't change array!
}
// arr is still {1, 2, 3}
```

2. **No index access:**
```java
for (String name : names) {
    // Can't print index!
}
```

3. **Forward only** - Cannot iterate backwards

4. **Cannot skip elements** - Visits every element

**When to Use:**
- Reading array values
- Calculating sum/average
- Searching for elements
- Displaying all elements

**When NOT to Use:**
- Modifying array elements
- Need element indices
- Iterating backwards
- Skipping elements

