**Loop Selection Guide:**

**1. for Loop** - Known number of iterations
```java
// WHEN: You know iteration count in advance
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

// WHEN: Iterating over array indices
for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
    array[i] *= 2;
}
```

**2. Enhanced for Loop** - Array/collection iteration (read-only)
```java
// WHEN: Just reading values, no modification
for (int num : numbers) {
    sum += num;
}

// WHEN: Displaying all elements
for (String name : names) {
    System.out.println(name);
}
```

**3. while Loop** - Unknown iterations, condition-based
```java
// WHEN: Continue until condition met
while (!done) {
    done = processNextItem();
}

// WHEN: User input validation
int number = 0;
while (number <= 0) {
    number = scanner.nextInt();
}
```

**4. do-while Loop** - At least one execution needed
```java
// WHEN: Must execute once before checking
do {
    displayMenu();
    choice = scanner.nextInt();
    processChoice(choice);
} while (choice != 0);

// WHEN: Prompt before validation
do {
    password = getPassword();
} while (!isValid(password));
```

**Decision Tree:**
```
Need to execute at least once?
├─ Yes → do-while
└─ No → Continue

Know number of iterations?
├─ Yes → for loop
└─ No → Continue

Just reading array values?
├─ Yes → enhanced for
└─ No → while loop
```

**Summary:**
- **for**: Counter-based, known iterations
- **for-each**: Read-only array iteration
- **while**: Condition-based, unknown iterations
- **do-while**: At least once, then check condition

