**Input Validation with Loops:**

**Scenario:** Get a valid age (1-120) from user

**Approach 1: while Loop**
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class InputValidation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int age = 0;

        while (age < 1 || age > 120) {
            System.out.print("Enter age (1-120): ");

            if (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
                age = scanner.nextInt();

                if (age < 1 || age > 120) {
                    System.out.println("Invalid! Age must be 1-120.");
                }
            } else {
                System.out.println("Invalid! Please enter a number.");
                scanner.next();  // Clear invalid input
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Valid age entered: " + age);
        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

**Approach 2: do-while Loop (Better)**
```java
Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
int age;
boolean valid = false;

do {
    System.out.print("Enter age (1-120): ");

    if (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
        age = scanner.nextInt();

        if (age >= 1 && age <= 120) {
            valid = true;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Age must be 1-120!");
        }
    } else {
        System.out.println("Please enter a number!");
        scanner.next();  // Clear invalid input
    }
} while (!valid);

System.out.println("Valid age: " + age);
```

**Why do-while is Better:**
- Prompts at least once (natural flow)
- Clearer separation of prompt and validation
- More readable logic

